# Scheduled monitor: {{MONITOR_NAME}}

Parent session is pure orchestration: acquire lock → spawn one subagent → release lock.
It does **no** message reading or replying itself — all that lives in the subagent.

## 1. Acquire lock

Run Bash:

```bash
/home/claude/workspaces/bin/slack-lock acquire {{MONITOR_NAME}}
```

- `LOCKED` → prior run still active (its polling overran this fire).
  **Skip entirely**: spawn nothing, emit nothing, end turn.
- `ACQUIRED` → proceed to step 2.

## 2. Spawn exactly one subagent

Use the Agent tool (`general-purpose`) to monitor Slack channel **{{CHANNEL_NAME}}**.
Give it **only** this task:

> Read `{{PROJECT_DIR}}/SLACK_CRON.md` **in full first**, then follow it exactly — it is
> your complete playbook (when to reply, hand-off, disclaimer, polling). Do all message
> reading / reasoning / replying in your own context via Slack MCP (ToolSearch:
> `mcp__claude_ai_Slack__slack_read_channel`, `mcp__claude_ai_Slack__slack_send_message`).
>
> Parameters for this monitor:
>
> | param | value |
> |-------|-------|
> | channel | {{CHANNEL_NAME}} (`channel_id={{CHANNEL_ID}}`) |
> | project dir | `{{PROJECT_DIR}}` |
> | monitor name (for `slack-lock refresh` while polling) | {{MONITOR_NAME}} |
> | worker (for hand-off nudge) | {{WORKER_NAME}} |
> | tasks file (hand-off queue) | `{{TASKS_FILE}}` |
> | notes file (monitor's own memory) | `{{NOTES_FILE}}` |
>
> Return only terse status (`silent`, `replied:N`, `queued:N`, or combos).

## 3. Release lock

After the subagent returns, **always** run Bash (even if it errored):

```bash
/home/claude/workspaces/bin/slack-lock release {{MONITOR_NAME}}
```

## Parent rules

- Aside from `slack-lock` acquire/release, emit **nothing** — no narration, no summary.
- Discard the subagent's returned status.
